1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,928
Good afternoon.

2
00:00:00,928 --> 00:00:06,000
We are Group 19, Track 1, and this is ThreadPilot, the general manager’s co-pilot.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,472
Our user is the general manager of a quick-response knitwear factory.

4
00:00:09,472 --> 00:00:17,975
She runs it on the move, without SQL, and the questions start at seven: did anything go wrong yesterday, how is the TrendCart order, can we take 800 hoodies by the 25th?

5
00:00:17,975 --> 00:00:25,723
Today that costs an estimated seventy-three planner-hours a month, and a wrong answer costs a delivery: eight of thirty-four open orders are already late.

6
00:00:25,723 --> 00:00:30,000
ThreadPilot answers from the factory's own records, and acts only after she confirms.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,399
User journey. 07:00 — the general manager opens ThreadPilot before walking the floor. The day is already on one screen.

8
00:00:33,399 --> 00:00:37,002
RED: 8 orders overdue, assembly below its normal range for 6 days, 3 decisions waiting. Nothing here was asked for.

9
00:00:37,002 --> 00:00:41,600
A decision card: ORD-029 will miss 1 April. “Review options” runs the queue simulation.

10
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:47,973
Moving ORD-029 up saves it 5 days but makes 6 other orders 11 days later in total, so it recommends keeping the current order.

11
00:00:47,973 --> 00:00:54,384
It also drafts before she asks: both stalled orders already have a chase-up, written from the records.

12
00:00:54,384 --> 00:01:00,741
“Review & send” opens the preview: to washing, 9 days idle, 3 days overdue. She checks it and confirms.

13
00:01:00,741 --> 00:01:06,048
Sent and logged; ORD-002 leaves the list. Nothing went out before her click.

14
00:01:06,048 --> 00:01:08,038
Every chart has an “i”: what it shows and why it is computed that way.

15
00:01:08,038 --> 00:01:11,839
“Normal” = the previous 8 same weekdays: on this factory that cut false alerts from 0.67 to 0.1 a month.

16
00:01:11,838 --> 00:01:19,039
The morning briefing, written at 07:00, each point with its supporting records.

17
00:01:19,039 --> 00:01:24,919
09:40 — on the floor, she asks in her own words.

18
00:01:24,919 --> 00:01:32,209
TrendCart has six active orders, so it asks instead of guessing.

19
00:01:32,209 --> 00:01:36,267
ORD-005: 500 scarves at knitting, due 20 April. Each answer shows the tool calls behind it.

20
00:01:36,267 --> 00:01:41,256
“Show more”: the planner’s reading, each tool with its arguments, and the records it used.

21
00:01:41,256 --> 00:01:46,519
11:15 — a customer asks for 800 hoodies by the 25th.

22
00:01:46,519 --> 00:01:54,340
It asks the one thing that changes the answer: ready at the factory, or delivered?

23
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:59,742
An estimate with its assumptions: the queue’s finish date, the on-time chance, the bottleneck, outside workshops that could take it. Not a promise.

24
00:01:59,742 --> 00:02:06,774
Core task, end to end. 14:30 — the same chase in her own words, typed the way it is said on the floor.

25
00:02:06,774 --> 00:02:15,993
It checks the claim against the record first — last activity 24 March, 8 days: true — then drafts to knitting.

26
00:02:15,993 --> 00:02:21,613
Preview first. Nothing is sent until the manager confirms this exact draft.

27
00:02:21,613 --> 00:02:29,555
Sent from the manager’s mailbox, and logged.

28
00:02:29,555 --> 00:02:37,214
A conditional watch: at 10:00 tomorrow it notifies only if no reply has been recorded.

29
00:02:37,214 --> 00:02:43,215
It also speaks first: an alert email only for a decision needed today — at most 3 a day, never the same item twice in 24 hours.

30
00:02:43,215 --> 00:02:48,998
Hard case 1 — a wrong premise: ORD-029 “stuck since last week”.

31
00:02:48,998 --> 00:02:53,399
The record says it moved today. It says so, and asks before writing anything.

32
00:02:53,399 --> 00:02:58,696
Hard case 2 — no order ID, only a description.

33
00:02:58,696 --> 00:03:05,970
Three orders match, so it lists them instead of picking one.

34
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:10,171
“The vest one” narrows that list to ORD-120 and drafts to assembly. Nothing sent.

35
00:03:10,171 --> 00:03:15,596
Hard case 3 — an order that does not exist.

36
00:03:15,596 --> 00:03:20,598
Not in the records — and it says so. It does not invent an order, a stage or a number.

37
00:03:22,167 --> 00:04:00,167
We tested against ground truth: seventy new questions written from the data files only, every answer computed twice and checked, run once with no fixes. Sixty-nine percent fully correct, with a confidence interval of fifty-seven to seventy-eight. Production and normal-range questions: ten of ten. Not-in-the-records: seven of eight. But rankings and comparisons: three of eight each. Without the language model, twenty-one percent. With eight to ten questions per type, read the categories as indicative. And in thirteen hundred graded runs, no email went out without a confirmed preview.

38
00:04:00,167 --> 00:04:27,167
What breaks it? Nine misses need a ranking or filter we never built, like oldest by order date. Seven are order numbers it can't read, like hash eighty-one, or eighty-three or two. Three comparisons give a table but no verdict. The pattern: one question, one tool, it is right. Next we build one sort-and-filter tool, fix the number parsing, and run these seventy on the live model.

39
00:04:27,167 --> 00:04:49,167
We built a co-pilot a manager can use all day, and on questions it had never seen it was right sixty-nine percent of the time, never sent an email without a confirmed preview, and never invented a record. What we learned: a score on questions you tuned against is a regression check, not accuracy; rules belong in code; and the misses cluster, so build the missing tool first. Thank you.
